1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:04.023
CrossSource: measuring whether a RAG system can be trusted to cite the law

2
00:00:04.623 --> 00:00:16.561
An open evaluation harness for citation accuracy in legal RAG — built to answer one question with rigor: when the system cites a court opinion, is the claim actually supported by that source?

3
00:00:17.161 --> 00:00:18.770
Items: 150.

4
00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:20.540
Opinions: 45.

5
00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:22.033
Topics: 15.

6
00:00:22.313 --> 00:00:25.975
Rater kappa: 0.98 and 0.84.

7
00:00:27.175 --> 00:00:28.200
Why this exists

8
00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:35.536
In Mata v. Avianca, lawyers were sanctioned for filing a brief full of citations an AI invented.

9
00:00:35.816 --> 00:00:44.989
Citation hallucination is the canonical trust failure of legal AI — and it is exactly the class of failure my day job revolves around.

10
00:00:45.269 --> 00:00:58.570
CrossSource is the public, reproducible version of that work: a dataset where every item traces to an exact passage in a public opinion, a written labelling protocol, and an evaluation pipeline anyone can run.

11
00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:00.391
Method

12
00:01:00.991 --> 00:01:20.282
Version 0.3 dataset: 150 items, 75 Q A citation and 75 citation check, over 45 public U S opinions from Court Listener, 27 federal circuit and 18 state supreme court, 2018 or later, across 15 legal topics.

13
00:01:20.562 --> 00:01:27.465
Provenance: every item carries its opinion ID, URL, the exact passage and the cited authority.

14
00:01:27.745 --> 00:01:32.691
Labelling: a written protocol, the labelling protocol file, version 1.1.

15
00:01:32.971 --> 00:01:37.605
Items are model-drafted and model-checked; no human labelled every item.

16
00:01:37.885 --> 00:01:40.900
Agreement: two blind, independent model raters.

17
00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:47.555
Cohen's kappa 0.98 on citation check, 0.84 on Q A citation.

18
00:01:47.835 --> 00:02:00.363
Adjudication: every disagreement plus a 10 percent audit sample, 30 items in all, adjudicated by me with a third independent model rater. 27 of 30 matched the recommended label.

19
00:02:00.643 --> 00:02:16.000
Version 0.1 corpus: 22 public-domain U S court opinions, 16 Supreme Court, 2 federal circuit and 4 state, sourced from Harvard Law School's Caselaw Access Project and chunked to about 350 words.

20
00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:20.637
Version 0.1 retrieval: BM25, top 5.

21
00:02:20.917 --> 00:02:29.506
Version 0.1 generation: Claude, with mandatory inline citations in doc ID, colon, chunk ID format.

22
00:02:29.786 --> 00:02:37.352
Version 0.1 golden set: 25 human-authored questions with verified ground-truth supporting chunks.

23
00:02:37.632 --> 00:02:48.246
Version 0.1 configurations: baseline, which is citation formatting only, versus strict, which adds explicit citation-discipline rules to the prompt.

24
00:02:48.526 --> 00:02:59.656
Version 0.1 scoring: claim-level citation precision and recall, faithfulness, and answer relevance, scored by an LLM judge and validated by hand.

25
00:03:00.856 --> 00:03:01.603
Findings

26
00:03:02.203 --> 00:03:08.012
Table 1, a version 0.3 sanity run on a 30-item sample, not a full run.

27
00:03:08.292 --> 00:03:20.157
Claude Sonnet 5.5: citation precision 0.919, citation recall 0.718, citation check accuracy 1.00.

28
00:03:20.437 --> 00:03:31.868
GPT 5 mini: citation precision 0.763, citation recall 0.718, citation check accuracy 0.93.

29
00:03:32.468 --> 00:03:37.947
Table 2, version 0.1, 25 questions: baseline versus strict.

30
00:03:38.227 --> 00:03:44.541
Citation precision: 0.981 in baseline, 0.994 in strict.

31
00:03:44.821 --> 00:03:48.385
Citation recall: 0.760 in both.

32
00:03:48.665 --> 00:03:51.891
Faithfulness: 1.000 in both.

33
00:03:52.171 --> 00:03:58.408
Answer relevance: 0.980 in baseline, 0.960 in strict.

34
00:03:59.008 --> 00:04:05.553
Table 3, version 0.1, 25 questions: failure taxonomy by configuration.

35
00:04:05.833 --> 00:04:09.617
Right document, wrong passage: 3 in baseline, 1 in strict.

36
00:04:09.897 --> 00:04:11.907
Missing authority: 6 in both.

37
00:04:12.187 --> 00:04:14.232
Wrong document: 0 in both.

38
00:04:14.512 --> 00:04:16.676
Unsupported claim: 0 in both.

39
00:04:17.276 --> 00:04:32.922
The v0.1 finding that still stands (n = 25): citation-discipline prompting buys precision — wrong-passage citations drop 3 → 1, precision rises 0.981 → 0.994.

40
00:04:33.202 --> 00:04:44.744
It cannot buy recall: 0.760 in both configurations, capped by retrieval, because every missing-authority failure traces to retrieval, not generation.

41
00:04:45.024 --> 00:04:49.646
Knowing which layer owns the failure is the entire point of separating the metrics.

42
00:04:50.846 --> 00:04:53.446
The judge caught a bug — because I checked the judge

43
00:04:54.046 --> 00:05:01.192
In v0.1, I hand-graded a blind, stratified sample of the judge's citation-precision verdicts:

44
00:05:01.792 --> 00:05:04.699
100% agreement (15/15).

45
00:05:05.299 --> 00:05:17.016
That validation pass also surfaced a genuine harness defect — consecutive citations produced punctuation-only claim spans that the judge initially mislabeled as failures.

46
00:05:17.296 --> 00:05:18.947
Fixing it changed the metrics.

47
00:05:19.547 --> 00:05:29.234
That is the lesson worth paying for: an unvalidated eval reports wrong numbers with full confidence — precisely the failure mode the eval exists to catch.

48
00:05:30.434 --> 00:05:31.651
What this demonstrates

49
00:05:32.251 --> 00:05:34.372
The same discipline I run in production:

50
00:05:34.972 --> 00:05:36.288
Instrument the pipeline.

51
00:05:36.568 --> 00:05:39.472
Build a failure taxonomy instead of a single score.

52
00:05:39.752 --> 00:05:41.419
Validate the judge externally.

53
00:05:41.699 --> 00:05:46.087
Separate precision from recall, so you know whether to fix prompting or retrieval.

